Marta (support): Heads-up — customer ACME seeing TypeScript/Node SDK streaming hang in Next.js Edge functions. Client waits forever after ~6s of tokens, then client-side retry fires and we end up double-billing tokens. Need repro and quick mitigation.
Kevin (eng-runtime): Thanks. Which SDK version + exact runtime (Next target)? Any logs from the edge function? :mag:
Marta (support): package.json shows "@redwood/inference": "^1.3.2". Next 13.4, deployment on Vercel edge runtime (Edge Functions). App uses the TS SDK via server component in edge runtime. Error log from client side: "UnhandledPromiseRejection: TypeError: res.body.getReader is not a function". Customer code: ```ts
const res = await client.stream(...)
for await (const chunk of res.body) { /* append to UI */ }
``` They also pasted raw network capture showing SSE-ish fragments, sometimes split mid-line.
Ashley (acct-ACME): Confirming. Sometimes stream emits a few chunks, then stops; network tab shows a long idle gap, then a small final chunk. If we refresh we get duplicate early tokens and charged twice.
Tom (devex): Likely a mismatch: edge runtime expects Web ReadableStream; older Node-targeted SDKs might return a Node Readable or an adapter that doesn't fully behave in the V8 edge environment. Also SSE framing + gzip/proxy buffering can create long flush gaps. Do we have response headers (content-type, transfer-encoding, content-encoding)?
Marta (support): Headers from Vercel trace: `content-type: text/event-stream; charset=utf-8`, `transfer-encoding: chunked`, `content-encoding: gzip`. Also customer uses a custom proxy on path that adds gzip sometimes.
Kevin (eng-runtime): gzip + chunked + edge can be tricky — gzip blocks can be emitted in large buffers until full window. If SDK assumes per-token flush and the runtime/proxy re-chunks, the app-level loop can see long pauses. Also `getReader is not a function` suggests the object returned is not a Web Stream in that SDK build. Can you paste the exact SDK import line?
Marta (support): They do `import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference'` and `const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })` in an edge server component.
Tom (devex): Two quick things to try: 1) confirm they are using the edge-build of the SDK (we ship a web/edge-targeted bundle). Ensure install `@redwood/inference-edge` or upgrade to `@redwood/inference@1.4.0` which exposes a `createEdgeClient` or auto-detects. 2) temporary mitigation: consume as a generic fetch stream using `response.body?.getReader()` guard or fall back to `const reader = (res.body as any)?.getReader ? res.body.getReader() : new ReadableStreamDefaultReaderShim(res.body)` (we can share a tiny shim). That avoids throwing and prevents retry loop.
Ashley (acct-ACME): We tried updating to `1.4.0` yesterday and saw improvement but still hit rare stalls on longer outputs (>500 tokens). The `getReader` error went away after the upgrade.
Kevin (eng-runtime): Ok good. That rules out pure API mismatch. Next suspect: buffering in the Vercel edge/proxy or gzip blocking. Can you get raw chunk timings from the server side? We added light instrumentation to the SDK to log `Date.now()` when each chunk is emitted server-side — can you enable `REDWOOD_STREAM_TRACE=1` and re-run? Also capture the browser network stream raw events.
Marta (support): Customer enabled trace env var and pasted server logs — gaps of ~5–7s between successive chunk emits, not consistent. Browser network shows initial chunks arrive promptly, then a 6s gap, then small last chunk.
Tom (devex): That pattern smells like intermittent backpressure: either the encoder waits to flush a gzip window or the proxy aggregates chunks. Two actionable items: 1) temporarily disable gzip between SDK and customer origin (set `content-encoding: identity`), 2) force the SDK to flush after every token via `client.stream({ flushEveryToken: true })` flag (we can add server-side opt). Both will increase bandwidth but should reduce stalls.
Kevin (eng-runtime): I'll escalate to eng-runtime to instrument kernel selection for streaming paths and add logs for kernel flush times + time spent in compression. If we can reproduce on Vercel edge with a small script we can confirm whether it's gzipping/VC layer. Tom — can you put together a tiny repro using Next edge function that streams 800 tokens and a minimal client capturing raw chunks and timings?
Tom (devex): Yep I'll repo that and share. Quick repro plan: Next edge function that proxies /stream -> uses `createEdgeClient().stream()` and flushes each chunk with `await streamController.enqueue(...)` delays. Run on local `vercel dev` and on Vercel. I'll include both `gzip: on` and `gzip: off` variants.
support-bot: Incident created: INC-2026-1183 (priority P2) — escalated to eng-runtime. :rotating_light:
Ashley (acct-ACME): Appreciate the triage. Do you have a safe workaround to avoid duplicate billing while we wait for a fix? We can short-circuit retries client-side but worried about UX.
Marta (support): Recommend: 1) add client debounce on retry: wait 10s before re-requesting same chat. 2) set server-side token-idempotency header if supported to detect duplicate runs. 3) temporarily turn off gzip/proxy-level buffering. We'll add these to the support note.
Kevin (eng-runtime): For root cause tracking: eng-runtime will: (A) reproduce on Vercel edge with gzip on/off, (B) add detailed chunk-timestamp traces in next SDK release, (C) consider adding `flushEveryToken` opt in SDK to force small chunk flushes (perf tradeoff). ETA: initial repro + logs by end of day.
Marta (support): I'll share repro steps and the customer's logs in the INC-2026-1183 thread and notify ACME. Thanks all.
Tom (devex): Sharing quick shim snippet for consumers to avoid `getReader` crash:
```ts
function safeReader(res: any) {
  if (res?.body?.getReader) return res.body.getReader();
  // Node-style stream fallback
  const nodeStream = res.body;
  const reader = {
    async read() {
      return new Promise(resolve => {
        const chunk = nodeStream.read();
        if (chunk != null) resolve({ done: false, value: chunk });
        nodeStream.once('readable', () => resolve(this.read()));
        nodeStream.once('end', () => resolve({ done: true }));
      });
    }
  };
  return reader;
}
```
Marta (support): Nice. Adding that to the support ticket. Closing here until eng-runtime posts repro logs. :white_check_mark:
